why no-entrainment locking works: for the equal pair, psi_f + psi_b = -S*d/C IDENTICALLY
(phase-independent).  To first order the kernel force depends only on that sum, so the
restoring force toward dstar is branch/split independent; the seed split (psi_f=0) merely
freezes a gate offset (joint gate < 1) without moving the equilibrium or the stiffness.
One-sided entrainment (the specified thj pull) converges to the psi_f=0 branch, NOT the
symmetric branch; a symmetric split needs two-sided entrainment.  All three settings agree
on d_inf to O(1e-4).

CRITICAL SIGN CHECK: in every run (both seeds, mix = none/0.01/0.3, with and without
repulsion) the kernel bond term dd = -kappa*base*Sm*dVdd evaluated at CURRENT phases
pushed d TOWARD dstar at every step (0 violations above threshold): the bond is a
two-sided restoring force under realistic phase dynamics.

==============================================================================
T4 -- vacuum inertness + crumb bound
==============================================================================
Sm = sqrt(mi_eff*mj_eff), mi_eff = sqrt(mob_i*max(mob_j,fl0)), mj_eff = sqrt(mob_j*max(mob_i,fl0))
  => Sm^2 = sqrt(mob_i*mob_j) * sqrt(max(mob_i,fl0)*max(mob_j,fl0));  the factor sqrt(mob_i*mob_j)
     vanishes when EITHER mob is 0, so Sm = 0 EXACTLY for any vacuum partner.  fl0 = 0.01
  numeric: Sm(0, 0.8125) = 0    Sm(0.8125, 0) = 0
  crumb pair Em_i = Em_j = eps:  Sm(eps) = sqrt(eps*max(eps,fl0)) ; for eps < fl0 this is
  sqrt(eps*fl0)  (proportional to sqrt(eps) -- the fl0 floor BOOSTS small crumbs above linear).

